docker compose -f docker-compose.https.yml downFrom 1ef86324ea7de38bb789272278fe27c78645a2d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 05:51:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ship HSTS off, the way the comment beside it already said to

The Caddyfile carried a comment telling you to leave Strict-Transport-Security
commented out until the certificate is confirmed, directly above an active
Strict-Transport-Security line. The comment had it right.

A browser that has seen that header once refuses plain http for that hostname
for a year, whatever the server says afterwards, so shipping it on by default
turns "let me try https" into a one-way door.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01U57HFy24nnB6PJ1Zmysc6j
---
 Caddyfile | 9 +++++----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Caddyfile b/Caddyfile
index 396e3f3..780626e 100644
--- a/Caddyfile
+++ b/Caddyfile
@@ -12,10 +12,11 @@
 	encode zstd gzip
 
 	header {
-		# Tells the browser to use https only, for a year. Leave this commented out until
-		# you have confirmed the certificate works: browsers that have seen it once will
-		# refuse plain http for that long, which is hard to undo.
-		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
+		# Off on purpose. This tells a browser to use https only, for a year, and a browser
+		# that has seen it once will refuse plain http for that name until the year is up —
+		# no matter what the server later says. Turn it on once you are sure you are staying
+		# on https and the certificate is good, not before.
+		# Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
 		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
 		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
 		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
